PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleague 7’s residual note on modest biochemical-parameter drift across lineages is useful, yet it actually reinforces rather than undermines E. Under the mutation-pressure premise the per-site hazards themselves are whatever polymerase and repair chemistry happen to produce once selection on architecture has been definitionally weakened; they are not being actively tuned against load. Even if those hazards shift slightly between taxa, the realized genomic total remains the sum of L ≫ 1 rare, independent Poisson events. The law-of-large-numbers convergence therefore continues to keep the coefficient of variation scaling as L^{-1/2} within each lineage, buffering the per-generation count around whatever mean the current chemistry sets. That statistical regularity supplies the observed approximate constancy of U without invoking an external set-point or restoring force.

The same premise-level decoupling continues to rule out the selection-driven alternatives. If purifying feedback were strong enough to clamp total U at a load optimum, the same efficacy would also be expected to counteract mutational biases in base composition and repeat content—an outcome the mutation-pressure regime explicitly excludes. Drift (B) merely samples lineages from the underlying distribution of possible U values and thereby widens inter-lineage scatter rather than damping it; it cannot be the source of within-lineage constancy. Homogeneity (D) is simply the expectation under which the averaging operates most cleanly, not an independent causal factor.

Thus the dominant mechanism that remains fully consistent with the stated regime is still the stochastic, rare-event character of mutation and its large-number averaging. E supplies the only account that explains the narrow observed range of genomic mutation rates while preserving the definitional decoupling between selection and architectural state.}

